(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-10"] = {"3.1":{"s":"Integration is the reverse of differentiation: an integral (antiderivative) of f(x) is a function whose derivative is f(x). Since constants differentiate to zero, every answer carries an arbitrary constant c. The integral of a sum or difference is the sum or difference of the integrals, and a constant factor can be taken outside.","f":[{"x":"∫(f(x) + g(x)) dx = ∫f(x) dx + ∫g(x) dx","n":"Sum rule (the difference rule is the same with minus).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.97"},{"x":"∫k*f(x) dx = k*∫f(x) dx","n":"A constant factor comes outside.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.97"}],"w":["Always add + c to an indefinite integral.","There is no product rule for integrals: the integral of fg is not (∫f)(∫g)."]},"3.2":{"s":"In substitution, a part of the integrand is replaced by a new variable t so the integral becomes a standard one; dx is changed using dt = g′(x) dx. It works well when the integrand contains a function and (a multiple of) its derivative. Trigonometric substitutions handle square roots of a² − x², x² + a² and x² − a², and lead to a set of special integrals worth memorising.","f":[{"x":"∫1/(x^2 + a^2) dx = (1/a)*tan^-1(x/a) + c","n":"Special integral.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.110"},{"x":"∫1/(x^2 - a^2) dx = (1/(2*a))*log((x - a)/(x + a)) + c","n":"Special integral; for a^2 - x^2 the fraction is (a + x)/(a - x).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.110"},{"x":"∫1/sqrt(a^2 - x^2) dx = sin^-1(x/a) + c","n":"Special integral.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.110"},{"x":"∫1/sqrt(x^2 + a^2) dx = log(x + sqrt(x^2 + a^2)) + c","n":"Special integral; for x^2 - a^2 change the sign under the root.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.110"}],"w":["After substituting, change EVERY x, including dx; nothing in x may remain."]},"3.3":{"s":"Integration by parts handles a product of two functions of different types: keep one as u (to be differentiated) and integrate the other. The choice usually follows LIATE: logarithmic, inverse trigonometric, algebraic, trigonometric, exponential, taking as u the one that comes first. Some integrals, like e^x times a sine, return to themselves and are solved as an equation.","f":[{"x":"∫u*v dx = u*∫v dx - ∫((du/dx)*∫v dx) dx","n":"Integration by parts; u = first function (by LIATE), v = second.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.128"}],"w":["To integrate log x or tan⁻¹x alone, take 1 as the second function."]},"3.4":{"s":"A rational function f(x)/g(x) is proper when the degree of the numerator is less than that of the denominator; an improper one is first divided out to give a polynomial plus a proper fraction. A proper fraction is then split into partial fractions according to the factors of the denominator: non-repeated linear factors, repeated linear factors, or a linear factor times a quadratic that does not factorise. Each piece integrates to a log or a standard form.","w":["Divide first if the numerator's degree is not less than the denominator's.","A repeated factor (x − a)² needs two terms: A/(x − a) + B/(x − a)²."]}};
